## Interface: 16001
## Title: Forever STUwave
## Notes: Neon synthwave-styled nameplate health bars and player/target unit frame recolors, violet-to-pink and cyan gradients.
## Version: 0.1.0
## Author: parker
## SavedVariables: ForeverSynthwaveDB, ForeverSynthwaveErrorLog
# ACCOUNT-WIDE ONLY, and that is a client bug workaround, not a preference.
#
# The Forever beta client WRITES SavedVariablesPerCharacter correctly and never
# restores them -- a known bug that resets every addon's settings on login.
# Measured here rather than assumed: a boot counter read "boot #1" on every
# login while the file on disk plainly held boot = 1, absent at file scope, at
# ADDON_LOADED and at PLAYER_LOGIN alike. Blizzard's own PTR Issue Reporter
# loses its saved position to the same bug, which is why that widget re-centres
# every reload.
#
# Account-wide ## SavedVariables DOES restore on this client. Proven by running
# both side by side for one reload: "perChar boot #1 | account boot #2".
#
# So anything that must survive a reload goes in ForeverSynthwaveDB. The
# per-character declaration is gone entirely rather than left as a decoy --
# leaving it would invite the next person to store something there and watch it
# silently vanish.
#
# Revisit if Blizzard fixes the loader; per-character is the better home for
# per-character data.
# The other eight diagnostic dumps (Probe, GridDiag, ScaleDiag, ShownFrames,
# DataBarRegions, MinimapKids, ButtonRegions, MemDiag) were declared here too,
# and the whole list stopped RESTORING -- every variable came back empty while
# still being written correctly on unload. Proven with a boot counter that read
# "boot #1" on every single login, and by the .bak file holding a real captured
# position the next session never saw.
# Re-add one temporarily when a dump is actually needed, rather than keeping
# eight debugging leftovers permanently on a line that has to work.
#
# ForeverSynthwaveErrorLog is back on the line as of 2026-09-22, and it is not
# one of those temporary dumps. It is the ONLY channel the dev loop has: an
# undeclared global is never written at unload, so fsdev.py's read_log had
# nothing to parse and printed a clean bill of health on every run. "No errors"
# and "errors are not being saved" are the same output, which is the worst
# possible failure mode for the one tool that is supposed to tell us the truth.
# fsdev now warns when this global is missing from the file, so if it is ever
# dropped from this line again the loop says so instead of going quiet.
#
# Note what this declaration needs, which is less than the comment above
# assumes: the error log only has to be WRITTEN on unload, never RESTORED. A
# broken restore costs nothing here -- a fresh empty log each session is what
# we want anyway.

# LibActionButton-1.0 and friends stay in libs/ for reference but are NOT
# loaded: secure snippet bodies do not execute on this client, and that
# library assigns type/action inside a snippet, so its buttons would render
# permanently empty. See the header of ActionBars.lua for the measurement.

# NOTE 2026-09-21: the block below was committed in 4272e3d with two comment
# markers missing, so `ErrorLog.lua and` was left as a LOADABLE ENTRY. WoW
# skips a .toc entry whose file is absent, so nothing broke, but the client
# was being asked to load a file literally named "ErrorLog.lua and".
#
# The intent was the community ForeverSVFix technique: a junction at
# SVData\ForeverSynthwave.lua that gets the client to load ErrorLog.lua and
# everything after it, working around the broken per-character loader.
#
# That junction has never existed -- not in the repo and not in the deployed
# addon folder -- so the line below was inert. It is commented out rather
# than deleted because the technique is still the fallback if the
# account-wide workaround above ever stops working. To use it, create the
# junction first, then uncomment.
# SVData\ForeverSynthwave.lua

ErrorLog.lua


